; expect 12
; PARAMETERISED STRUCTS, and the capability record they exist for.
;
; A struct may name type parameters the way a `data` does, in the same
; parenthesised spelling: `(struct Box (a) (v : a))`. Before this the
; form was AX2001 - "expected identifier, found `a`" - and an interface
; had to be a `data` type destructured by `match`, because only a
; struct has named fields and `.field` access.
;
; WHY THE FIRST GROUP IS AMBIGUOUS HERE AND NOT IN A `data`. A data
; declaration's other groups are CONSTRUCTORS, which start with an
; uppercase name, so `(a)` can only be parameters. A struct's other
; groups are FIELDS, which start lowercase too:
;
;     (struct Span (start : Int) (end : Int))
;              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a field, not a parameter list
;
; The discriminator is the `:`, and it is the same one in both the
; compiler (`structGroupIsTyParams`) and the tree-sitter grammar
; (a dynamic precedence on `type_parameters`, which `(x : Int)` cannot
; match anyway). Handing the group to `collectTyParams` unguarded
; swallowed `Span`'s first field and every struct in the tree stopped
; parsing - the seed refused to compile `self_host/main.ax`, which is
; where that belongs.
;
; What the terms hold:
;
;    4  two parameters stay INDEPENDENT. `(Pair 7 "seven")` binds `a`
;       to Int and `b` to String through one shared instantiation env,
;       so a variable used by two fields is one placeholder in both and
;       this cannot answer `(Pair Int Int)`.
;    8  a capability record: a struct holding a FUNCTION, selected by
;       the value's own type, called through `.field`. This is what
;       replaces a trait.
(import Str)

(import Fmt)

(struct Pair (a b)
  (fst : a)
  (snd : b))

(struct ShowOf (a)
  (render : (-> a String)))

(:: showInt (ShowOf Int))

(fn (showInt) (ShowOf fmtInt))

(:: b2i (-> Bool Int))

(fn (b2i x)
  (if x
    1
    0
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let ((p (Pair 7 "seven")) (c showInt))
    (+
      ; 4 - the two parameters are independent: `fst` is an Int the
      ; arithmetic below accepts, `snd` a String only strLen accepts.
      (* 4 (b2i (&& (== p.fst 7) (== (strLen p.snd) 5))))
      ; 8 - the capability answers through its field
      (* 8 (b2i (strEq ((c.render) 7) "7")))
    )
  )
)
